@SYS
.
=SYS
.
.SYS a calls the machine code at address a. The address is any UWORD
.expression: a constant, a variable or @name.
.
:DIM REG_A AS UBYTE AT $030C, REG_X AS UBYTE AT $030D
:DIM REG_Y AS UBYTE AT $030E, REG_P AS UBYTE AT $030F
:REG_A = 65
:SYS $FFD2                    ' CHROUT prints A
.
.  - Before the call SYS loads A, X, Y and P from $030C, $030D, $030E and
.    $030F. After the call it stores the four back there. X16 ROM BASIC does
.    the same.
.  - DIM ... AT names the four bytes, as in the example.
.  - SYS ANDs P with $F3, so D and I are clear during the call.
.  - Carry is bit 0 of REG_P. IF REG_P AND 1 THEN PRINT "FAILED" tests the
.    carry a call returns.
.  - The four bytes are not cleared when the program starts. Set what the
.    call reads.
.  - The called code may change X. SYS keeps the program's X, which indexes
.    the expression stack.
.  - A SYS to a constant address is 7 bytes of code. The routine behind it,
.    43 bytes, is in the program once.
.  - A string address stops the compile with TYPE MISMATCH.
.
.See ASM, Declarations.
.
=SEE ALSO
.
